  "full_review": "What’s a good cop to do when he heads a team that is able to arrest a killer before he actually kills, only to find himself pursued as an imminent murderer? This is the question, along with larger societal issues, that fuels Steven Spielberg’s engrossing “Minority Report” (20th Century Fox).\n\n����������� In the Washington of 2054 the murder rate is down to an astounding zero thanks to the combination of technology and three psychics called Pre-Cogs who are kept sedated in a pool and envision upcoming murders. Their visions are projected onto a screen enabling the police department’s Pre-Crime unit, led by Detective John Anderton (Tom Cruise), to rush to the site and arrest the would-be killer.\n\n����������� The program is so successful it is about to go national, much to the satisfaction of its creator, Anderton’s boss, Lamar Burgess (Max von Sydow). The fly in the ointment is Anderton’s rival, Danny Witwer (Colin Farrell), and when a vision of an upcoming murder shows Anderton as the killer, Witwer is fierce in pursuing him as the distraught cop tries to evade capture and prove his innocence.\n\n����������� The elaborate plot thickens when Anderton discovers the system may be flawed. In this Big-Brother type society where people are identified by eye-scan even to take a subway, Anderton undergoes a hasty eyeball exchange operation, after which he kidnaps the Pre-Cog named Agatha (Samantha Morton) who is key to finding out the truth -- and perhaps what happened to his young son, snatched from his side six years earlier.\n\n����������� Spielberg’s direction is masterful. He reins in the cast, getting understated performances from Cruise and Farrell especially, who are all the more effective for it. The visuals are compelling as well, sometimes grainy, often bullet-fast in the violent sequences, and never resorting to the usual camera angles or perfunctory exposition.\n\n����������� There are big-budget, special-effects action scenes typical of a summer popcorn movie, but the narrative also presents issues of societal good at the cost of personal freedom, and the value of human life. This is particularly evident in the exploitation of the Pre-Cogs who were born drug-addicted and confined to a near comatose state once their psychic gifts were discovered. The movie is also topical in questioning how far government should be allowed to go to protect its citizens from potential criminals, a front and center issue for Americans today.\n\n����������� Spiritual flourishes also crop up throughout the story: Witwer was a seminarian and compares cops to clergy; a cop rushing to duty makes the Sign of the Cross; the Pre-Cog’s pool is called a temple and prisoners wear a device called a halo. Perhaps the inclusion of these spiritual allusions is meant to counterbalance the futuristic scenario which hints at government dominating personal lives.\n\n����������� Based on a short story by Philip K. Dick, the film blends science fiction, suspense and neo-noir, shot in “bleached” cinematography that gives it just the right futuristic and unsettling look as Anderton struggles to stay one step ahead of the law.\n\n����������� The murder mystery is not exceptional, however, and one can guess a few developments, which means the pace can seem halting at times even if the images are always enticing. Occasional bursts of sly humor, such as when Anderton’s original eyeballs go rolling down the hallway, liven up the film’s intensity. In sublimely creepy scenes mechanical spiders leap onto the faces of ordinary citizens to identify them by eyeball scanning; Gideon (another Biblical reference) the warden keeps prisoners in a kind of suspended animation; and the black market surgeon (Peter Stomare) who operates on Anderton leaves him blindfolded to feast on moldy food.\n\n����������� On the other hand, there is a great deal of product placement of brand names throughout the film. It’s funny when Anderton enters the Gap and is greeted by the Japanese name of the man whose eyeballs he wears, but several close-ups of his Bulgari watch and other obvious trade names seem unnecessary.\n\n����������� “Minority Report” should be a major success, transcending escapist entertainment through its attention to the humanity of its characters and questions of social justice.\n\n����������� Because of recurring stylized sci-fi violence, brief substance abuse, fleeting sexual situations, occasional profanity and an instance of rough language, the U. S. Conference of Catholic Bishops classification is A-III -- adults. The Motion Picture Association of America rating is PG-13 -- parents are strongly cautioned. Some material may be inappropriate for children under 13.\n\n- - -\n\n����������� Pare is director of the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops’ Office for Film and Broadcasting."
